WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.930
- Welcome, welcome into another
edition of "Market Takes".

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:05.280
I know you could have
been anywhere in the world

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.830
but you are here with me
and I appreciate that.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.890
I'm Dion Rabouin, reporter
at the "Wall Street Journal".

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.150
Today we are digging

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.388
into the latest existing
home sales report,

00:00:14.388 --> 00:00:17.040
talking about and
explaining what's going on

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.080
with the housing market right now.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.660
Existing home sales we know are about 80%

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:22.860
of the overall housing market.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.510
So as we talk through this report

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.400
we're gonna talk through
the big picture in housing.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.620
We're gonna talk about what
the report said, obviously,

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.690
and how that compared to expectations.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:31.890
Then we're gonna talk about how this fit

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.020
with other recent housing
reports that we've seen.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.810
We talked a little bit about
this on Monday's look ahead,

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.460
looking forward to this week's data.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:39.540
We're gonna talk about how this fits

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.520
with the current Inflation narrative.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.770
Take a little look at what's
going on in the markets.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.600
And then talk about what comes next.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.460
Because this report and how it fits

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.290
into everything else that's going on

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:50.760
is gonna tell us some important things

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.320
about what's going on with the economy

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.290
and also what's going on with
the overall housing market.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.640
So let's get into that.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:57.930
First things first, I want to talk

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.000
about today's report and
what the report said.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.370
So wanna throw up here
a chart on your screen

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:03.960
so you can just see for yourself

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.880
what we've got going
on in the markets now.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.920
As you see there, those last four reports,

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.140
you see there was a clear
trend moving downward

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.120
and then the numbers started to pick up

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.950
and those bars get a little bit higher

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.540
as we moved into 2023.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.690
So for May, you saw
4.3 million homes sold.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.580
That's that last bar there on your screen.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.950
Existing home sales rose 0.2%.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.320
They were expected to drop 0.9%.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.850
So economists were expecting

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.190
that declining trend that we had seen

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.290
coming into this year to pick up again.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.080
And we had seen those two
reports in a row come negative.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.330
But this really reverses that trend.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:37.920
And we saw home sales rise slightly.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.490
We also saw prices rise just
a little bit to $396,000.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.120
The average existing home
sold for about $396,000,

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.000
up from $395,000 a month ago.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.840
Now, one thing that you're gonna
hear a lot about from folks

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.810
is this was the biggest
one year drop since 2011.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.870
But if you look up there on
your screen and you go back

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.170
about a year, get to the
middle of that 2022 number,

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:04.650
well you see where those bars are

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.030
isn't really that different.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.220
The decline was in prices,

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.110
and the decline in
terms of number of sales

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:10.943
wasn't that great.

00:02:10.943 --> 00:02:12.570
So this chart really just illustrating

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:13.830
that we are seeing, again,

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.670
a pickup in the number of used homes sold

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.260
or the number of existing homes sold,

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.210
and we are not seeing the continuation

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.620
of that declining number.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.210
That was what the Fed wants to see.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.190
We'll get into why the
Fed wants to see that

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:27.330
in just a moment.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:28.830
When we talk about prices, right,

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.800
there was the biggest
drop in prices since 2011.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.750
That's what I was referring
to a little bit earlier.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.390
The biggest year over year drop in prices.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.700
But that year over year drop in prices

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.400
was from $408,000 to $396,000.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.134
Think about it, if you're
going out to buy a home,

00:02:43.134 --> 00:02:44.790
that's not a big difference.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.830
It's only about a 3.1% drop.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.720
It's just because generally speaking,

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.470
home prices have not declined
since 2011 very much.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:56.760
So a 3.1% drop is the biggest
drop we've seen since 2011.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:57.870
That should just tell you, listen,

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:59.520
that's not really a big deal.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.200
And that's a year over year decline.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.070
So in one year, prices drop
3.1%, you kinda shrug that off

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.020
if you're someone who's
thinking about buying a home

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.370
or especially if you're
someone who's thinking

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.980
about selling a home, that
really doesn't matter to you.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.050
The momentum stalling
out here really suggests

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.490
that we could have hit a floor.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.590
Now, what the Fed wants is for home sales

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.320
to continue to drop because
it makes rent prices drop.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.570
With those higher rates, with higher rates

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:24.240
of about double where
they were a little more

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.460
than a year ago or a year and a half ago

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.530
there is an expectation that
you're gonna see, you know,

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.150
home sales falling off a cliff.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:31.110
That hasn't happened.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.690
They've declined a lot
from those huge highs,

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:37.170
from those big highs we
saw in 2021 and into 2022.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.690
But you're not seeing it fall off a cliff.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.360
We're still above four
million sales per month.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.490
So that's a big deal.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:45.930
Also, the decline in the number

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:48.630
of homes sold year over year
seems to be flattening out.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.330
Now this is 20% lower
than the number of homes

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:54.060
that were sold a year ago, but again,

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.320
that trend is flattening.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:00.090
So in May, 2022, there's
about 20% less homes sold

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.370
in May '23 than there were in May '22,

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.890
but that's about the same as
the previous three months.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.320
You saw a 23% decline the month before,

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:08.997
22.1% the month before,

00:04:08.997 --> 00:04:12.330
and 23.1% decline the month before that.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:15.360
All that to say it was
just rocketing lower.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.660
You saw declines each month
getting bigger and bigger,

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.510
down to a 37% year over
year drop at the beginning

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.010
of the year in January.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:24.240
And that's reversed course

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.520
and now we're only about a 20% drop.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.290
So again, it's lower.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:29.820
There are less sales, certainly,

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.027
but the decline looks to be tapering off.

00:04:32.027 --> 00:04:35.640
All that to say that we
have just really seen

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.530
a flattening out of this trend.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.360
It's not picking back up yet,

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.620
but there are reasons to believe

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:41.670
that that could be happening

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.320
when we look at some of the other data.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:44.520
Now earlier this week we got

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.495
the NAHB Housing Market Index
and housing starts data.

00:04:48.495 --> 00:04:51.660
Both of those reports came in
much stronger than expected.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:52.950
Wanna put up here on your screen

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.440
the NAHB Housing Market index.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.080
That came in this past
month beating expectations,

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.260
and the trends you see on
your screen now is that index.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:04.380
And that's an index of
sentiment as well as sales.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.350
And it factors in new
home sales a lot more.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.210
But this is really about
home builders, right?

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:10.680
Are folks going out and
building more homes?

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:11.970
Are they excited to build homes?

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:15.240
And as you can see there,
there is a clear pickup

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.250
and we've seen it since
the start of this year.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.680
Every month you've seen
since the start of this year

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:20.880
that index has picked up,

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.400
and now we're back at the
highest level that we have seen

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.220
on that index since May of 2022.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.620
You also have seen new home
sales start to pick up.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.860
And this is why maybe you
could start seeing a bit

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.260
of a renaissance in the housing market

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:35.970
if this trend starts to pick up.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:38.460
And this chart is really illustrating

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:41.700
that rising sentiment that
we've seen so far this year

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.830
in the housing market in terms of,

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.080
especially that new home sales market.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.420
Housing starts were
actually expected to decline

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.310
but rose by 22%.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.990
So a huge increase in the number of houses

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.620
that are going to be built.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.110
Contracts are are laid out
there and folks saying, okay,

00:05:58.110 --> 00:05:59.790
we're gonna go out and build a new house.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:02.790
Those jumped by 22% last month,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:05.070
expected to decline and
we got instead a reversal.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.870
So when we look at the new home sales,

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:10.287
up about 12% year over year
and about 4% on the month.

00:06:10.287 --> 00:06:14.430
683,000 homes sold so that
gives you an idea of the scale.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.230
Only about 700 new homes sold

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:19.380
compared to 4.3 million
existing homes sold.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:23.130
That shows you there's a
lot more existing homes

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.630
than new homes to be sold.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.360
We look at April's big
month over month gain,

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.210
that's evidence that this
construction of new homes

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:31.770
is really starting to pick up.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:32.790
But that's largely

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:35.130
because there's just
very little inventory.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.020
Inventory is still at historic lows

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:38.970
in the existing housing market.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.340
The sale of new homes is
about seven and a half months.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.080
You got about seven and a half months

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:46.170
of the normal pace of sales for new homes.

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:49.380
For existing homes it's
at just three months.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:53.130
And to give you an idea of
historically where that is,

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:56.040
that number of existing homes supply

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:59.400
peaked at about 12 months
of supply back in 2010.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.830
So in 2010, we're coming
off the, you know,

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.290
the 2008 global financial
crisis, which was set off

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.060
by the mortgage market crisis

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.420
and the big busts you had
there, and really a lot of homes

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:10.950
were being listed and put on the market

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.390
and there weren't a lot of buyers.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:16.560
Well, we had a record low
of 1.7 months of supply

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.570
in January of 2022, that's the low.

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:21.450
So we're up from the low
at three months right now,

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.760
but still very far away from that high

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:27.090
of 12 months of supply
that we saw in 2010.

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.730
And really between 2010 and 2022,

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.250
you just saw that market just
kind of decline in terms of

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:35.160
the amount of months
of supply on the market

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:36.810
that just really kind of fell.

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:40.170
And we got from that 12
number to that 1.7 number,

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:42.420
basically two months of
supply on the market.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:45.990
We are seeing a continued
trend of higher housing sales

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:48.963
on the new home side, but
we are not seeing that

00:07:48.963 --> 00:07:50.520
on the existing home side.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.680
We're seeing a flattening out.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.810
NAR, the National Association of Realtors

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:57.180
Chief Economist Lawrence
Yun said something

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:58.290
I thought was really interesting

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.300
in today's existing home sales report.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.670
He said, quote, "Newly
constructed homes are selling

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:05.910
at a pace reminiscent
of pre pandemic times

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:08.610
because of abundant
inventory in that sector.

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:12.060
However, existing home sales
activity is down sizably

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.000
due to the current
supply being roughly half

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.830
of the level of 2019."

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:19.590
So really what we're seeing
is a problem of supply,

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:22.680
not enough supply, but
still lots of demand.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:23.910
That's not what the Fed wants.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:25.830
The Fed wants to reduce demand.

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:28.050
The Fed's goal is to make you and I,

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.700
people who might want to
go out and buy a home,

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:31.860
say you know what, man,
I wanna buy a house,

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:34.050
but these rates are too high.

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:36.060
Oh, it's gonna cost me
so much money to go out

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:37.620
and get a mortgage, I'd rather not.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:39.060
I'd rather just rent, you know,

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:41.310
or stay in my current place where I am.

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:42.930
Well, that doesn't seem to be happening.

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:45.630
There still is a lot of
demand out there for housing

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:46.980
and there's just not supplies.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:48.630
So folks are still going out looking,

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:50.250
they're still on Zillow,
they're still scrolling,

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:51.570
they're still going out and you know,

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:54.570
hitting those open houses
and things like that.

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:55.530
But they're just not buying

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.240
because they're not
finding anything that is

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.340
in their price range or that they like

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:00.330
or that they can afford,

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:02.250
because you know, rates are so high.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:03.930
But also home prices are still high.

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:07.590
We're down 3.1% from what was
near record highs a year ago.

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:10.050
So again, we're still
seeing all the problems

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:13.020
that were existing in the
housing market continue.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.240
And now we're starting to
see those existing home sales

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:17.730
level off and not fall anymore.

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:20.220
That could play a big
role in overall inflation.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:21.990
So when we talk about inflation

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:23.700
we've gotta take a look at the CPI,

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:25.110
the Consumer Price Index.

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:27.690
Wanna put that chart up here
on your screen right now.

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:30.150
So when you're talking about the trend,

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:31.890
and that's what always matters,

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:33.660
you see there from the beginning of 2021,

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:34.710
and I wanted to compare this

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:38.340
to that existing home sales
chart that I showed you before.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:39.480
It just rises up.

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:42.330
And then since that peak
in the middle of 2022

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:43.770
you see there on your screen,

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:45.600
inflation has just continued to come down.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.840
It's consistently declined
every month really since then.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:50.850
And you see that nice wave pattern down.

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.080
That's not what you see

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:53.910
from the existing home sales report.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:56.460
That's showing it comes down,
but then it picks up again

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:58.260
and now we're plateauing
at that higher level.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:09:59.850
Wanted to just show the CPI chart

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.130
to illustrate the way
that inflation has moved

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:04.860
and how that's different from
overall existing home sales.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:07.710
Housing is the biggest
component of core CPI,

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:11.610
which has held above 5%.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:14.220
Housing is about 40%
of that core CPI number

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:15.420
that the Fed watches.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.750
So really this is telling
us right now that inflation,

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:21.390
while it's been coming down consistently,

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:23.370
the trend that we are seeing in home sales

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:25.980
is not going along with
that and falling as well.

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:28.140
And we could be seeing a leading indicator

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:29.520
of inflation stalling out

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:32.010
